{"id":"2dcd0d8a-4119-42d7-9925-b7b3011098c5","object":"chat.completion","created":1785018825,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    \\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    \\n    symbol_count = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n[\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n        symbol_count[sym] = symbol_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    \\n    removable_candidates = [n for n in nodes if n[\\\"removable\\\"] and n[\\\"parent\\\"] is not None]\\n    removable_candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[\\\"depth\\\"], x[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n    \\n    removed = set()\\n    removed_order = []\\n    \\n    for node in removable_candidates:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        # Cannot remove if any child is retained\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid not in removed for cid in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        # Check required symbols preservation\\n        sym = node[\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_count[sym] == 1:\\n            continue\\n        # Proceed to remove\\n        removed.add(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n        symbol_count[sym] -= 1\\n    \\n    retained = [n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes if n[\\\"id\\\"] not in removed]\\n    retained.sort()\\n    \\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to understand the task. It refers to \"SE-PE-01\" which seems to be about grammar-guided node reduction, likely based on Perses syntax-guided reduction. Given the input structure: nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list.\n\nThe task: implement solve(case) that returns {\"removed_node_ids\": [...], \"retained_node_ids\": [...]}. The function must decide which nodes to remove and which to retain, based on the described algorithm.\n\nFrom the description: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\"\n\nBut the provided code context is minimal. We have to infer the algorithm from the instructions and the fixture data.\n\nKey points:\n- \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes by depth descending.\n- \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So cannot remove root (depth 0, symbol root, removable false?). Also nodes whose removal would remove a required symbol. Also cannot remove node if any of its children are retained (i.e., if we haven't decided to remove the child yet? Actually \"a node with retained children\" means we cannot remove a node if some of its children are retained. So we need to process bottom-up.)\n- \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So before removing a node, check if after removing it, all required symbols are still present in the tree (i.e., there is some node with that symbol remaining). So we need to track which symbols are present.\n- \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So among nodes at same depth, order by node ID (string? likely lexicographic). But we process deeper first, then by depth descending, then by depth? Actually \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So sort by depth descending. For same depth, use node ID for stable order.\n- \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed? \"actual removal order\" suggests the order in which we tentatively remove nodes (and they are actually removed). retained_node_ids should be sorted (alphabetically? probably lexicographically).\n- \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts: grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties.\" So we must ensure grammar validity? But the grammar is not provided. Perhaps it's about the structure of the tree? The nodes have parent pointers forming a tree. \"Grammar-valid\" might refer to the tree being a valid parse tree according to some grammar, but since we don't have grammar rules, maybe it's about not removing nodes that would make the tree invalid? Possibly not; the description mentions \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar\" but without parser generation. Possibly the algorithm is: we try to remove nodes that are removable and whose removal does not affect the set of required symbols. Additionally, we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained (i.e., if we keep child, we must keep parent). This is typical in tree pruning: you remove a node only if all children are also removed (or if it's a leaf). But the instruction \"a node with retained children\" suggests we cannot remove a parent if any child is retained. So essentially, we can only remove a node if all its descendants are also removed (or if it's a leaf and removable and not required). But we also have the condition that after removal, all required symbols must still be present. So we need to check if the node being removed contributes a required symbol that would become missing.\n\nAlso \"removable\" flag: only nodes with removable=true can be considered for removal. But the instruction says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So even if removable, we can't remove if it violates those.\n\nAlso \"required_symbols\" list: which symbols must be present in the final tree. So we need to ensure that after all removals, each required_symbol appears at least once in the retained nodes.\n\nWe have to output removed_node_ids in removal order (the order we decided to remove them). And retained_node_ids sorted (presumably alphabetically by id? Probably by id string lexicographic order).\n\nWe need to implement solve(case) that does this.\n\nLet's design algorithm:\n\n1. Build a map from node id to node dict.\n2. Build children mapping: for each node, collect list of child ids (by scanning parent field).\n3. Determine initial set of symbols present (each node contributes its symbol).\n4. Process nodes in order: sort by depth descending, then by node id string ascending as tiebreak (stable ties).\n5. For each node in that order (starting from deepest):\n   - If node is root (depth=0) or not removable, skip.\n   - If node's symbol is required and that symbol appears only in this node (i.e., count of that symbol in current retained set is 1) then cannot remove because it would lose the only occurrence of a required symbol. But careful: \"required symbols\" are a list of symbols that must remain represented. So if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, and if that symbol's count in the tree (excluding this node?) would become zero after removal, then we cannot remove it. However, it might be that the symbol appears elsewhere, so even if this node is removed, the symbol might still be present. So we need to check if after removal, each required symbol still has at least one node.\n   - Also, we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained (i.e., have not been removed). Since we process bottom-up, children are processed before parent (depth descending). So when processing a node, its children have already been considered. If any child was retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove this node. So we need to keep a set of removed node ids, and for each node, check if any child id is not in removed set. If so, skip.\n   - If both conditions satisfied, tentatively remove the node: add to removed node ids list (in order), and update symbol counts (decrement count for its symbol). Also note that after removal, we should not consider its descendants again (but they are processed before, so already decided). Also note that removing a node might affect ancestors later (fewer children retained).\n6. After processing all nodes, determine retained node ids: all nodes not removed. Sort them lexicographically (string sort).\n7. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order_list, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained_list}.\n\nBut there is nuance: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That means if a child is retained, parent cannot be removed. That is consistent.\n\nAlso \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we must check before removal that after removing this node, the required symbols are still present (i.e., each required symbol has at least one node not removed). So we need to maintain a count of each symbol among retained nodes. Initially, count each symbol. When we remove a node, decrement count for its symbol. Before removing, check if decrementing would make any required symbol count zero (i.e., if that symbol is required and count is 1). If so, skip.\n\nBut also, what about nodes that are not removable? They are never removed. So retained set initially includes all nodes. We only consider removable nodes for removal.\n\nImportant: The order of processing: deeper nodes first. This ensures that when we consider a parent, its children have already been decided. If any child was retained (i.e., not removed), then parent cannot be removed. If all children were removed, then parent can be removed if also removable and not required (or its symbol still appears elsewhere). This is typical.\n\nBut what if a node has no children? Then it's a leaf. Condition \"a node with retained children\" - does not apply if no children. So leaf can be removed if removable and required symbols still present.\n\nAlso root is depth 0, not removable, so never removed.\n\nNow, the fixture shows \"removable\" field for each node. Some are true, some false. Also \"required_symbols\": [\"root\",\"s1\"]. So root symbol must remain, and s1 must appear. The tree includes multiple nodes with symbol s1. So we can remove some s1 nodes as long as at least one remains.\n\nNow, we need to implement solve(case) in Python using only standard library.\n\nWe must return a JSON object with keys removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nWe should also handle any edge cases.\n\nNow, about the output schema: \"additionalProperties\": false, so only those two keys.\n\nWe'll write the code.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The depth field may be used but we can also compute from parent pointers. But using depth is simpler.\n\nAlso need to be careful about the \"parent\" field: null for root. Build children list.\n\nWe'll assume that the node list is a complete tree, all parent references exist.\n\nImplementation steps in solve:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    # Build maps\n    node_map = {}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        node_map[nid] = n\n        children[nid] = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n[\"parent\"]\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n[\"id\"])\n    # Symbol counts\n    symbol_count = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n        symbol_count[sym] = symbol_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    # Process removable nodes in order\n    # First, collect all removable nodes (excluding root)\n    removable_candidates = [n for n in nodes if n[\"removable\"] and n[\"parent\"] is not None]\n    # Sort by depth descending, then by node id ascending\n    removable_candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[\"depth\"], x[\"id\"]))\n    removed = set()\n    removed_order = []\n    for node in removable_candidates:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        if nid in removed:\n            continue\n        # Check if any child retained (i.e., not removed)\n        has_retained_child = any(cid not in removed for cid in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Check if required symbols would be preserved\n        sym = node[\"symbol\"]\n        # If this symbol is required and its count is exactly 1, then removing would lose it\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_count[sym] == 1:\n            continue\n        # Also, we should not remove root, but root is not removable anyway.\n        # Proceed with removal\n        removed.add(nid)\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n        # Update symbol count\n        symbol_count[sym] -= 1\n    # retained nodes: all nodes not removed\n    retained = [n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if n[\"id\"] not in removed]\n    retained.sort()  # lexicographic sort\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained\n    }\n\nBut we need to confirm if the order of removal matters for tie-breaking. The instruction says \"registered_candidate_order\" and \"stable_ties\". Our sorting by depth then id yields a deterministic order. That should be fine.\n\nAlso, we must ensure that we don't remove a node that is required symbol if it is the only one, but also what if the node's symbol is not required but its ancestor is? That is irrelevant. The requirement is only about required symbols remaining represented.\n\nAlso, \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" - that is implied by the check.\n\nOne more nuance: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The root is depth 0, and its parent is null, and removable false. So handled.\n\nBut what about a node that is a required symbol? That is covered by the symbol count check: if its symbol is required and count is 1, we skip. If count > 1, we can remove it because it's still present elsewhere.\n\nIs there any other condition? Possibly \"grammar_valid\" might require that the tree remains a valid parse tree according to some grammar. But without grammar, we assume that removing a node only if all its children are removed (so no dangling children) maintains tree validity? Actually, if we remove a parent node, its children are also removed (since they are deeper and processed first; if they were retained, we would not remove parent). So if we remove parent, all its descendants are also removed (since they were removed earlier). That ensures the tree remains a set of connected subtrees? Actually, if we remove a node, we also implicitly remove its entire subtree because children are removed before parent. So the resulting graph is a forest? But the root is never removed, so we end up with a tree that is a subset of nodes. That seems plausible.\n\nThus algorithm is reasonable.\n\nNow we need to produce the implementation as a JSON string.\n\nThe output must be a JSON object with a single key \"implementation\" whose value is the Python source code as a string, properly escaped.\n\nWe need to include the function definition of solve(case). Use only standard library.\n\nWe'll write the code as a string.\n\nLet's write the code in a block, then convert to JSON string with escaped characters.\n\nWe'll also include necessary imports? No external imports needed.\n\nWe'll treat the case argument as dict.\n\nPotential issues: The node ids in the fixtures are strings like \"n1\", \"root\". Sorting retained by string should work.\n\nWe'll use double quotes for strings.\n\nNow, produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2057,"completion_tokens":3405,"total_tokens":5462,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2930},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":9},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}